  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nEarlier this morning, an area of weakening convection was \nmoving across north central Kansas as an upper level disturbance\nembedded in a 10-15 knot west/northwest flow crosses western \nKansas. A 500mb high remains in place across the southwest \nUnited States while an upper level trough was located over the \nGreat Lakes Region. At the surface a surface boundary extended \nfrom eastern Colorado to the Panhandle of Texas. \n\nModels today remain in good agreement and have run to run \nconsistency. These models show minimal movement of the surface \nboundary over the next 36 hours as the next subtle upper level \ntrough rotates around the 500mb high and approaches western \nKansas late today. As this upper level system moves out into \nthe west central high plains late today there will be a chance \nfor scattered afternoon thunderstorms over eastern Colorado and \nalong the surface boundary which will be located near the \nOklahoma Panhandle border which, if they develop, will move \nsoutheast. A few of the stronger storms early tonight may be \ncapable of producing wind gusts of 50 to near 60 mph. As for \nthe possible thunderstorms that may develop over eastern \nColorado late today...they weaken and cross extreme southwest \nKansas later tonight. \n\nFor the first few days of this upcoming work week the 500mb \nhigh over the southwest United states will move very slowly \nwestward which will result in placing southwest Kansas on the \nnortheast edge of the upper highs and in a northwest flow \nregime. Based on this southwest Kansas can look forward to \nmultiple opportunities for scattered thunderstorms as additional\nupper waves rotate out of the Rockies and into the Plains. As \nfor the storms on Monday...conditions are expected to be similar\nto those anticipated later tonight as the next upper level \ntrough approaches from the west. Isolated wind gusts of 50 to 60\nmph will be the main hazard before sunset across west of \nhighway 83. A better chance for thunderstorms is expected late\nTuesday as the next upper level trough moves across the Western\nHigh Plains. Latest ensembles continue to show strong consensus\non timing and track of this next upper wave. This upper wave \nwill be associated with the right entrance region of an upper \nlevel jet as it crosses the Northern Plains. Ahead of this \nTuesday upper wave the ensembles indicate now a 50-60% \nprobability of MUCAPE >500j/kg and bulk shear >30 knots over \nwestern Kansas along with southeasterly upslope flow of near 70 \ndegree dewpoints. \n\nAlong with the slight chance for severe thunderstorms on Tuesday\nthere will also be a chance for widespread beneficial rainfall\nto be possible for some areas of southwest Kansas from this \nTuesday night event based on the high PWATS the ensembles \nforecast across southwest Kansas as storms develop and moves \nsoutheast along a moist instability axis. Current PWAT values\nare forecast to be near 1.5 inches (roughly 80% of normal). In\naddition to the high PWATS the latest NBM guidance has a 50 to \n60% chance that rainfall totals Tuesday night will be greater \nthan 0.25 inches west of highway 283. \n\nThis upper level trough crossing western Kansas Tuesday night \nwill not only bring a +60% chance for thunderstorms to southwest\nKansas Tuesday night but it will also allow a cold front to \nmove into southwest Kansas on Wednesday. This cold front will \nbriefly bring an end to the hot (near 100 degree) temperatures \nacross southwest Kansas Monday and Tuesday. Highs mid week will \nbe more seasonal with highs mainly in the lower 90s. Latest NBM\neven has a 60-75% chance for highs to be less than 90 degrees \nalong the I-70 corridor and around the Garden City area.",
